Z||000003||mrfiero||02-01-2000||11:51 AM||||If you're only in for $100 I would let it go and chalk it up to experience.  Find yourself a nicer, better Fiero that doesn't need as much work.  It sounds like this one would have nickel and dimed you to death anyways.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||02-01-2000||12:05 PM||||Speed, mrfiero is absolutly right!  Let it go!  If it need's alot of work, cheep up front, ain't cheep overall!  A fella that works for me, bought a 84 se on an impulse (I told him, if he wanted a fiero to hold off), for $600.  In 6 months he has replaced; the heater core, computer (got ripped off on that), starter, battery, front brake roters, and the radiator pipe was cracked... I am guessing that he has spent around $1200 or more, fixing the car so far.  It still needs to have all the engine/trany mounts and bushings replaced, the right headlight doesnt work, and it need some body work and a paint job. <P>So, the point is... if it needs alot of work, and your looking for transportation... it ain't worth it!  However, if it needs a lot of work, and your building/restoring a dream car... it still may not be worth it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||PAUL88GT||02-01-2000||01:25 PM||||Letting the car go may not prevent the damage this car dealer may do to your credit rating. If you clear this up and get the car back, I suspect there would be no damage to your credit rating.  If you let it go, there is some chance the car dealer will get this loan default added to your credit report which will make it harder to get loans or buy on credit in the future. Just my $0.02. ||||reg||1
